  "query_preview": "..High pressure aloft over the southwest United States\nwill be the primary influence on northern Arizona weather through\nThursday. Look for dry conditions, with mild daytime temperatures\nrunning from 5 to 10 degrees above average. Low temperatures will\nbe near average due to the effects of a dry air mass, mainly \nclear skies and strong radiational cooling. Surface pressure \ngradients will favor generally light northeast of east winds at \n5-15 mph.\n\nFor Friday and the weekend...A pair of weak shortwave troughs will\nproduce chances for showers starting Friday, lasting through the \nweekend. The first shortwave will brush southern and southeast \nArizona with chances for showers across the eastern Mogollon Rim, \nWhite Mountains and Chuska Mountains from Friday afternoon into \nSaturday (where the best combination of upward motion and moisture\nwill reside). We're looking at trace to 0.10 inch accumulations \nwith the first shortwave. The second shortwave rapidly follows and\nbrushes the northern half of Arizona. Slight chances for showers \nwill spread across all of northern Arizona with the second\nshortwave as additional upward motion moves across the state. \nPrecipitation amounts of trace-0.20 inches are forecast with this \nsecond shortwave. Snow levels are expected to remain above 8,500 \nfeet with these two relatively mild systems. Otherwise, look for \ncooler daytime temperatures and partly cloudy to mostly cloudy \nskies. Winds becoming south to southwest 5-15 mph gusting to 20-25\nmph with the approach and arrival of the second shortwave.\n\nHeading into next week...Unsettled weather will continue as the\nstorm track remains in place. There is the potential for stronger\nand colder systems next week. The current best guess on timing \nfor the first of these stronger systems is next Monday into \nTuesday. We'll keep you posted as our confidence builds on the \ntiming and finer details.",
